e of ${(x+2y)}^{8},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=2y,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+2y)}^{8}=(\\frac{8}{0}){x}^{8}(2y)^{0}+(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}+(\\frac{8}{2}){x}^{8-2}(2y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{8}{8}){x}^{8-8}(2y)^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}y$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1} & =8{x}^{7}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =16{x}^{7}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ is $16.$" 13194139534281 true] [17592186101209 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534281 true] [17592186101209 1100 "${(x-2y)}^{6}=$" 13194139534281 true] [17592186101209 1100 "What is $(x - 2y)^{6}$?" 13194139534281 false] [17592186101209 1145 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-2y)}^{6},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-2y,$ $n=6.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-2y)}^{6}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2y)^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0})(x)^{6}(-2y)^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1})(x)^{6-1}(-2y)^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2})(x)^{6-2}(-2y)^{2}+… \\\\  & =1(x)^{6}+6(x)^{5}(-2y)+15(x)^{4}(4{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{6}-12{x}^{5}y+60{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283114 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1089 "q-113621" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1100 "The coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ in the expansion of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}$ is" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1113 #uuid "8f5f91ac-3706-5b6e-99d4-de4e4940a4d3" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283113 1145 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283115 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283116 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283117 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283118 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283119 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1041 "selection" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283119 13194139534281 true] [17592186283114 1110 #uuid "a1e9fa98-0a82-59da-8eb3-337bd9e3451a" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283115 1034 "1\\,680" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534281 true] [17592186283115 1111 #uuid "ccb44669-6c09-5452-8903-3da761de4065" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283116 1034 "210" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534281 true] [17592186283116 1111 #uuid "3d65452b-bf9c-5eef-a152-c7471af5d34e" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283117 1034 "420" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534281 true] [17592186283117 1111 #uuid "b7ae7c6f-bf08-5942-8736-6fafbec8d1b4" 13194139534281 true] [17592186283118 1